   "title": "Boeing (Douglas Aircraft Company), DC-9 Airplane Characteristics for Airport Planning (June 1984, revised F 5/84), 2.2 General Airplane Dimensions: Model DC-9-32; 2.7.4 Passenger Entrance and Service Door Clearances, Model DC-9",
   "accessed": "2026-11-24",
   "note": "Dimensions and outlines read off the DC-9-32 sheet's side, plan and front views (tools/trace/dc93/); the figures are the aircraft's measurements, not a copy of the drawing. The sheet is a 1-bit scan on graph paper (about 47 px a meter, so outlines are good to about 5 cm), drawn 0.43 degrees off level (turned level). Scaled by the printed 32.61 m (107 ft) from the nose to the tail cone; the plan and front views are at the side's scale. Against the sheet's printed figures: overall length 36.36 m (to the fin's top fairing's tail), span 28.44 m, tailplane 11.22 m, width 3.35 m, height 3.61 m and the engines 2.70 m out (see checks). The side and front views draw the tube within 0.8% of the printed 3.61 m, so it isn't fit to it. The JT8D nacelle (with its target thrust reverser and nozzle cone) is the sheet's, its fan cowl ending at 30.3 m. The sheet draws the cabin windows (34 solid, plus a dashed one at each end of the row, optional on some aircraft and left out), the left side's main entry door, the overwing exits and the right side's cargo doors (dashed); the right side's service door is placed from 2.7.4's partial plan (27 in wide, 4.51 m to its middle; its height the 48.5 in of 2.4's door list, its sill with the main door's). The main entry door's middle sits within 4 cm of 2.7.4's printed 172.5 in (4.38 m). The windows' height and width and the cockpit panes are the MD-80's (MD82: the same nose and cabin section), the windows' places along the body this sheet's. Confidence medium: a scan."
  }
 ],
